  <h2>Hanging Drywall: Horizontal vs. Vertical Orientation</h2>
  <p>
    How you orient your drywall sheets on wood or steel framing affects joint finishing time and structural rigidity:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Horizontal Installation (Recommended for Residential Walls):</strong> Running sheets horizontally spans across multiple studs, tying framing together and evening out irregular framing. It creates a single continuous waist-height joint around the room, which is far easier to tape and finish than tall vertical seams.</li>
    <li><strong>Vertical Installation (Standard for Commercial Steel Framing):</strong> On 8-foot or 9-foot ceilings where sheet lengths match wall heights exactly, vertical hanging places all edges on stud centers without butt joints. This is standard in commercial drywall with light-gauge steel studs.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Deducting Window & Door Openings (Rule of Thumb)</h2>
  <p>
    Standard practice among drywall contractors is <strong>not</strong> to deduct standard interior doorways (30″ × 80″) or single windows from total square footage. Drywall is hung directly over openings and routed out with a drywall cutout tool, meaning the cut-out section is effectively waste. Only deduct large openings such as 8-foot patio sliders, double garage doors, or two-story open foyers that exceed 40–50 square feet.
  </p>

  <h2>Fastener & Screw Spacing Requirements</h2>
  <p>
    Proper screw spacing prevents popped screws and sagging panels over time:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Ceiling Panels:</strong> Space drywall screws 12 inches apart along framing members (16″ or 24″ on-center).</li>
    <li><strong>Wall Panels:</strong> Space screws 16 inches apart along studs. For 5/8″ fire-rated drywall on walls, reduce spacing to 12 inches if required by local fire code.</li>
    <li><strong>Edge Margin:</strong> Fasteners should sit 3/8″ to 1/2″ in from panel edges to avoid breaking the gypsum core. Dimple the paper surface slightly without tearing the face paper.</li>
  </ul>
